Content-Type: text/plain;charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable



On Wed, Apr 3, 2019, at 11:22 PM, John Mattsson wrote:
> Hi Christopher,
>=20
> Thanks for the clarifications.
>=20
> >You=E2=80=99re correct that this requires both endpoints to adopt the=
 change=20
> >simultaneously. However, that does not contradict the quoted text, wh=
ich=20
> >states that the protocol is not changed.
>=20
> I think "this requires both endpoints to adopt the change simultaneous=
ly" is a problem as it makes it impossible to introduce this mechanism i=
n many existing deployments. I would expect a solution that can be intro=
duced gradually (some nodes at a time) in existing deployments supportin=
g RFC 8446 and/or RFC 5246.

I think perhaps my response was unclear. In your scenario, adding key im=
port support is essentially the same as adding a new PSK to a node. Some=
 nodes may offer (or accept) the PSK, while others do not. Successful us=
e of an imported PSK will only occur if both endpoints have imported the=
 same PSK. Gradual deployment is possible.

>=20
> But, if you want to change the behaviour of the using the same externa=
l PSK in both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 as allowed by RFC 8446, a solution not=
 requiring "simultaneous change" is probably not possible.
>=20
> The requirement that "this requires both endpoints to adopt the change=
 simultaneously" should probably be highlighted in the document.
>=20
> The current mechanism does not seem to be deployable for e.g. 3GPP unt=
il TLS 1.4 is introduced, and then only for TLS 1.4 and later... on the =
other hand, being limited to SHA-256 for PSK may be ok for 3GPP. (and 3G=
PP could just derive a Separate SHA-384 PSK if needed).
>=20
> Some more comment:
>=20
> - Which label should be used by various versions of DTLS?

Likely the same labels as the TLS counterparts, since the label more or =
less specifies the *handshake* variant.

>=20
> - "If a client or server wish to deprecate a hash function and no long=
er
> use it for TLS 1.3, they may remove this hash function from the set
> of hashes used during while importing keys. This does not affect the
> KDF operation used to derive concrete PSKs."
>=20
> If someone deprecates the use of a hash function inside TLS 1.3 (used =
by Transcript-Hash and HKDF), does it then makes sense to keep deriving =
keys outside of TLS with that same deprecated hash function?

No. If this deprecation occurs, then they would follow the advisory text=
 and not use the old hash when importing keys.

>=20
> - "To mitigate these problems, external PSKs can be bound to a specifi=
c hash function when used in TLS 1.3"
>=20
> I would change to "are bound to a specific". If the application does n=
ot specify anything, the keys are bound to SHA-256.

Works for me. I=E2=80=99ll file an issue to track the change.

Thanks,
Chris=20

>=20
> Cheers,
> John
>=20
>  -----Original Message-----
> From: Christopher Wood <caw@heapingbits.net>
> Date: Wednesday, 3 April 2019 at 16:25
> To: John Mattsson <john.mattsson@ericsson.com>
> Cc: "TLS@ietf.org" <TLS@ietf.org>
> Subject: Re: [TLS] Comments on draft-wood-tls-external-psk-importer-01=

>=20
>  Thanks for the feedback, John. I filed issues on the GitHub draft=20
>  located at [1]. Please see inline below for comments.
>=20
>  On 1 Apr 2019, at 12:00, John Mattsson wrote:
>=20
>  > Hi,
>  >
>  > Thanks for trying to solve this problem! Not having a way to use th=
e=20
>  > same external PSK for different cipher suites is definitely a thing=
=20
>  > missing from TLS 1.3.
>  >
>  > As I stated during the wg session, 3GPP have a few use cases that u=
se=20
>  > PSK-ECDHE between phone and core network. Contrary to what IETF peo=
ple=20
>  > sometimes believe, most 3GPP use of TLS (between phone and core=20
>  > network, between core nodes, and between different operators) use=20=

>  > mutual authentication with certificates.
>  >
>  > According to the current 3GPP specification, the phone and the core=
=20
>  > network would share an external PSK and an external identity. The h=
ash=20
>  > function is undefined, i.e. SHA-256 is used for TLS 1.3. The phone =
and=20
>  > the network would then negotiate TLS 1.2 with any cipher suite or T=
LS=20
>  > 1.3 with SHA-256.
>  >
>  > Some comments
>  >
>  > - The draft should make clear if the External PSK and external=20
>  > identity can be used together with the imported identities. I assum=
e=20
>  > the idea is that they can=E2=80=99t but I can=E2=80=99t see any tex=
t specifying=20
>  > this.
>=20
>  Indeed, that was the intent.
>=20
>  >
>  > - =E2=80=9DImported keys do not require negotiation for use, as a c=
lient and
>  > server will not agree upon identities if not imported correctly.
>  > Thus, importers induce no protocol changes with the exception of
>  > expanding the set of PSK identities sent on the wire.=E2=80=9D
>  >
>  > While I see how the defined mechanism can be deployed in a new=20
>  > deployment, I do not see how to introduce it in an existing=20
>  > deployment. Assuming that the external PSK cannot be used together=20=

>  > with the imported identities, the TLS client and server must introd=
uce=20
>  > the mechanism at the same time, that is often not possible to contr=
ol.
>=20
>  You=E2=80=99re correct that this requires both endpoints to adopt the=
 change=20
>  simultaneously. However, that does not contradict the quoted text, wh=
ich=20
>  states that the protocol is not changed.
>=20
>  >
>  > - =E2=80=9Cstruct {
>  > opaque external_identity<1...2^16-1>;
>  > opaque label<0..2^8-1>;
>  > HashAlgorithm hash;
>  > } ImportedIdentity;
>  > =E2=80=9D
>  >
>  > This scales poorly, A client has to send n*m identities where n is =
the=20
>  > number of supported versions and m is the number of supported hash=20=

>  > algorithms...
>=20
>  Yes, this is a tradeoff we made by opting to not modify the key=20
>  schedule.
>=20
>  >
>  > - Would it not be possible to just use the version number as label?=
=20
>  > I.e. 0x0304 instead of =E2=80=9Ctls13=E2=80=9D. In that way you do =
at least not=20
>  > have to define a new labels.
>=20
>  We probably could, though since the labels are subject to change (see=
=20
>  the TODO about possibly including the HashAlgorithm in the label), we=
=20
>  kept it as is.
>=20
>  >
>  > - =E2=80=9CTLS 1.3 [RFC8446] supports pre-shared key (PSK) resumpti=
on,=20
>  > wherein
>  > PSKs can be established via session tickets from prior connections =
or
>  > externally via some out-of-band mechanism.=E2=80=9D
>  >
>  > This seems to indicate that all use of PSK is resumption which is n=
ot=20
>  > correct. I suggest changing to =E2=80=9CTLS 1.3 [RFC8446] supports=20=

>  > pre-shared key (PSK) authentication=E2=80=9D
>=20
>  Good suggestion! We=E2=80=99ll fix it.
>=20
>  >
>  > - =E2=80=9Cimporting external PSK (Pre-Shared Key) into TLS.=E2=80=9D=

>  >
>  > Not sure if =E2=80=9Cinto=E2=80=9D is the right word, I think =E2=80=
=9Cfor=E2=80=9D as in th e=20
>  > title is better. The mechanism seems very much external to TLS itse=
lf.=20
>  > I see it as a mechanism to derive several external PSKs from a sing=
le=20
>  > external PSK.
>=20
>  Ditto.
>=20
>  >
>  > - =E2=80=9CSimilarly, TLS 1.2 and all prior TLS versions should use=
 "tls12"=20
>  > =E2=80=9C
>  >
>  > The versions prior to TLS 1.2 will soon be deprecated. And why spen=
d=20
>  > time updating the TLS 1.2 code base instead of moving to TLS 1.3?
>=20
>  We=E2=80=99re just covering our bases here. I tend to agree that the =
focus=20
>  should be on updating to TLS 1.3 instead of =E2=80=9Cpatching=E2=80=9D=
 TLS 1.2=20
>  stacks.
>=20
>  >
>  >
>  > Suggestion:
>  >
>  > Instead of deriving a large set of new external PSKs, wouldn=E2=80=99=
t it be=20
>  > possible to just use different hash functions in different parts of=
=20
>  > the key hierarchy? I.e. if SHA-256 is associated with the external =
PSK=20
>  > and SHA-384 is the hash algorithms in the chosen cipher suite:
>  >
>  > PSK -> HKDF-Extract(SHA-256) =3D Early Secret
>  > |
>  > +-----> Derive-Secret(., "ext binder" | "res binder", "")
>  > | =3D binder_key
>  > |
>  > +-----> Derive-Secret(., "c e traffic", ClientHello)
>  > | =3D client_early_traffic_secret
>  > |
>  > +-----> Derive-Secret(., "e exp master", ClientHello)
>  > | =3D early_exporter_master_secret
>  > v
>  > Derive-Secret(., "derived", "")
>  > |
>  > v
>  > (EC)DHE -> HKDF-Extract(SHA-384) =3D Handshake Secret
>  >
>  > Support of this could be signalled with the tls_flags extension. If=
=20
>  > the server does not support the extension it must according to RFC=20=

>  > 8446 chose a cipher suite with SHA-256.
>=20
>  Modifying the key schedule was discussed in the context of [2]. At th=
e=20
>  time, it seemed the desire to not touch the key schedule outweighed t=
he=20
>  desire to not inflate the ClientHello.
>=20
>  Best,
>  Chris
>=20
>  [1] https://github.com/chris-wood/draft-wood-tls-external-psk-importe=
r
>  [2] https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-davidben-tls-universal-psk-00
>=20
>=20
>=20

--843e2817e4cb4bdd8c3654245d697589
